    'social-media-strategy-for-small-business' => [
        'excerpt' => 'You do not need a huge budget or a full-time team. You need one clear audience, two or three platforms, a repeatable content mix and a monthly review. Here is the full plan.',
        'meta_title' => 'Social Media Strategy for Small Business: A Simple 5-Step Plan',
        'meta_desc' => 'A practical social media strategy for small business: pick one audience, two or three platforms, a simple content mix, a posting rhythm and a monthly review.',
        'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p class="answer"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> A good social media strategy for a small business has five parts: one clearly defined audience, two or three platforms, a simple content mix, a realistic posting rhythm and a monthly review of what worked. You do not need a big budget. You need consistency and a plan you can actually keep.</p>

<p>Most small business owners are told to "be everywhere" and "post every day". That advice burns people out within a month. The businesses that grow on social media are not the ones that post the most. They are the ones that show up consistently, say something useful to a specific group of people, and learn from the results. This guide walks through the exact steps we use with clients at Progix Digital, adapted so that a one-person team can follow them.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Define one audience before you write a single post</h2>
<p>The most common reason small business social media fails is trying to speak to everyone. When your content is aimed at "anyone who might buy", it connects with no one. Start by describing your best customer in one or two sentences. Who are they? What problem do they have when they find you? What do they already believe about your industry, and what do they get wrong?</p>
<p>A useful exercise is to look at your five best customers so far. What do they have in common? Their age or job matters less than their situation: a new parent looking for a trustworthy pediatric clinic, a restaurant owner tired of unreliable delivery, a start-up founder who needs a website quickly. Write that situation down. Every post you create should be useful to that person.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Choose two or three platforms, not seven</h2>
<p>Three platforms posted to consistently will always beat seven neglected profiles. Pick the platforms where your audience already spends time, and be honest about the type of content you can produce regularly.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Instagram and Facebook</strong> suit visual products, local businesses, restaurants, beauty, fitness and anything people buy on impulse or by recommendation.</li>
<li><strong>LinkedIn</strong> suits B2B services, consultants, recruiters, software companies and anyone selling to professionals.</li>
<li><strong>TikTok and YouTube Shorts</strong> suit businesses that can show products, behind-the-scenes work or quick tips on camera.</li>
<li><strong>Google Business Profile</strong> is not a social network, but for local businesses it works like one. Keep it updated with photos and posts.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are unsure, begin with one platform where your customers already are, and add a second only when the first has a steady rhythm. Our <a href="/services/social-media-marketing">social media marketing service</a> usually starts with three platforms for this reason.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Use a simple content mix</h2>
<p>A reliable formula keeps your feed useful without feeling like a constant sales pitch. A simple split that works for most small businesses is:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>40% educational content</strong> that helps your audience: tips, how-tos, checklists, short explainers and answers to questions customers ask you every week.</li>
<li><strong>30% proof</strong>: customer reviews, before-and-after results, case studies, testimonials and behind-the-scenes of real work being delivered.</li>
<li><strong>20% personality</strong>: your team, your story, your values, and the small moments that make your business feel human.</li>
<li><strong>10% direct offers</strong>: promotions, launches, bookings and clear calls to action.</li>
</ul>
<p>The educational and proof posts do the heavy lifting. They build trust, which is the reason someone eventually clicks the offer. If your feed is mostly offers, people scroll past it. If it is mostly personality with no proof, people enjoy it but do not buy.</p>

<h3>Turn customer questions into content</h3>
<p>The easiest source of ideas is your inbox and your phone. Every question a customer asks you is a post waiting to be written. "How long does delivery take?", "What is the difference between your two packages?" and "Can I change my booking?" are all valuable posts. Keep a running list and answer one each week.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Set a posting rhythm you can keep</h2>
<p>Consistency beats volume. A business that posts three times a week for a year will usually outperform one that posts daily for three weeks and then disappears. Decide on a rhythm that fits your capacity, for example three posts and a few stories per week, and protect it like a calendar appointment.</p>
<p>A monthly content calendar removes the daily "what do we post today?" stress. Once a month, sit down and plan the topics, write the captions, prepare the visuals and schedule everything in advance. Batch the work: one afternoon for photos or short videos, one for writing, one for scheduling. This is exactly how our team manages content for clients, and it is the reason the work stays consistent even when the business gets busy.</p>

<h3>Make every post easy to act on</h3>
<p>Tell people what to do next. A short, friendly call to action such as "save this for later", "send this to a friend who needs it" or "message us to book" turns passive viewers into active ones. Make sure your profile link, phone number or booking page is always up to date, because most of your new customers will check your profile before they contact you.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Review monthly and improve</h2>
<p>At the end of each month, look at three numbers: which posts were saved or shared most, which posts led to messages or clicks, and which topics got little response. Saves, shares and replies matter more than likes because they show real interest. Do more of what worked, and quietly drop what did not.</p>
<p>You do not need complicated software for this. A simple spreadsheet with the post topic, format, date and result is enough. After three months you will have a clear picture of what your audience actually wants, which is worth more than any generic best-practice list.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Posting without a goal.</strong> Decide whether a post is meant to educate, build trust or generate enquiries.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring comments and messages.</strong> Replying quickly is one of the cheapest ways to build trust and win customers.</li>
<li><strong>Copying big brands.</strong> Large companies have budgets and teams you do not. Be specific, local and personal instead.</li>
<li><strong>Buying followers.</strong> A small, engaged audience is worth far more than a large, silent one.</li>
<li><strong>Giving up too early.</strong> Social media compounds slowly. Give any new plan at least three months before judging it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>How much does social media management cost?</h3>
<p>Professional social media management starts at $199 per month with Progix Digital, which covers 20 posts across 3 platforms, a monthly content calendar and a performance report. Doing it yourself costs no money but takes several hours every week.</p>
<h3>How often should a small business post?</h3>
<p>Three posts per week is a realistic and effective target for most small businesses. Posting less often is fine if you stay consistent. Quality and regularity matter more than frequency.</p>
<h3>How long until social media brings customers?</h3>
<p>Expect to see engagement within the first month and steady enquiries after about three months of consistent posting. Paid promotion can speed this up, but the foundation is still good content.</p>

<h2>Putting it into practice</h2>
<p>A strong social media strategy for a small business comes down to focus: one audience, a few platforms, a useful content mix, a sustainable rhythm and a monthly review. Start small, keep going and let the results guide you.</p>
<p>Prefer to hand this over? Our <a href="/services/social-media-marketing">social media management packages</a> start at $199 per month and include the calendar, content, publishing and reporting. <a href="/pricing">See pricing</a> or <a href="/contact">book a free consultation</a> and we will recommend the simplest plan for your business.</p>
HTML,
    ],

