    'meta-ads-vs-google-ads' => [
        'excerpt' => 'Google Ads captures people already searching for what you sell. Meta Ads creates interest before people search. Here is how to decide where your first advertising dollar belongs.',
        'meta_title' => 'Meta Ads vs Google Ads: Which Should You Start With?',
        'meta_desc' => 'Meta Ads vs Google Ads explained: search intent vs discovery, costs, best use cases, a comparison table and how to choose where to spend your first ad budget.',
        'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p class="answer"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Start with Google Ads if people already search for what you sell, because it captures existing demand. Start with Meta Ads (Facebook and Instagram) if your product is visual, new or bought on impulse, because it creates interest before people search. Many businesses eventually use both.</p>

<p>Choosing between Meta Ads and Google Ads is one of the first big decisions in paid advertising, and the wrong choice can waste weeks of budget. The good news is that the right answer is usually obvious once you understand how each platform works and how your customers buy. This guide explains the difference in plain language and gives you a simple way to decide.</p>

<h2>Search intent versus discovery</h2>
<p>The core difference is <strong>intent</strong>. Google Ads shows your ad to someone who has just typed a question or a need into a search box, such as "emergency plumber near me" or "accounting software for freelancers". They are already looking, so they are closer to buying.</p>
<p>Meta Ads show your ad to people scrolling Facebook and Instagram. They were not looking for you, but you can interrupt their feed with something relevant: a striking image, a short video or an offer that fits their interests. Meta is a discovery platform. It is excellent at introducing your business to people who would buy if they knew you existed.</p>

<h2>Comparison at a glance</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Google Ads</th><th>Meta Ads (Facebook &amp; Instagram)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Customer mindset</td><td>Actively searching for a solution</td><td>Browsing, not yet looking</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Services and products people search for</td><td>Visual, new or impulse-buy products</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main ad formats</td><td>Search text ads, Shopping, YouTube, Display</td><td>Images, video, carousels, Stories, Reels</td></tr>
<tr><td>Targeting based on</td><td>Keywords and search behaviour</td><td>Interests, behaviours and audiences</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speed to enquiries</td><td>Often faster, because intent is high</td><td>Needs creative testing to find what works</td></tr>
<tr><td>Creative effort</td><td>Lower: mostly text and a good landing page</td><td>Higher: strong images and video matter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Progix management fee</td><td>From $299 per month</td><td>From $199 per month</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>When Google Ads is the better first step</h2>
<p>If customers regularly search for your service, Google Ads puts you in front of them at the exact moment they are ready to act. Think of emergency repairs, wedding venues, legal help, dentists, software tools and local services. In these markets, the buyer has already decided they need something and is comparing options.</p>
<p>Google Ads also gives you very clear data. You can see which keywords lead to calls, form submissions and sales, and you can pause the ones that do not. The trade-off is that popular keywords can be expensive, and your website or landing page has to do a good job of turning the click into an enquiry.</p>

<h2>When Meta Ads shines</h2>
<p>Meta Ads work well for visual products, local businesses, events, courses, fashion, food, beauty, real estate and anything people buy on impulse or by recommendation. It is also the better choice when you are launching something new that nobody is searching for yet, because there is no search demand to capture.</p>
<p>Meta also excels at <strong>retargeting</strong>: showing ads to people who have already visited your website or engaged with your page. These people already know you, so reminding them with an offer often brings a high return. The main requirement is good creative. A weak image or vague message will cost you money no matter how well the targeting is set up.</p>

<h2>How much should you spend?</h2>
<p>There is no universal number, because costs depend on your country, industry and competition. As a starting point, we suggest around $250 per month of ad spend for Meta and around $500 per month for Google, which gives the platform enough data to learn what works. Smaller budgets can still work if you target a narrow local area or a specific offer.</p>
<p>Remember that your ad spend is paid directly to Meta or Google, and it is separate from any management fee. You should always own your ad accounts so that your data and history stay with you.</p>

<h2>Start small and track everything</h2>
<p>Whichever platform you choose, install conversion tracking before spending anything. Without it you are guessing. Make sure you can measure the actions that matter to your business: phone calls, form submissions, bookings or purchases.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Set one clear goal</strong>, such as qualified enquiries or online sales.</li>
<li><strong>Begin with a modest budget</strong> and a narrow, well-defined audience or keyword list.</li>
<li><strong>Test a few variations</strong> of your message and creative, and let the data decide.</li>
<li><strong>Review weekly</strong>, pause what does not work and put more budget behind what does.</li>
<li><strong>Improve the landing page.</strong> A great ad that sends people to a slow or confusing page wastes money.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Should you use both?</h2>
<p>For many businesses the strongest approach is to combine them. Meta introduces your brand and fills the top of the funnel, while Google captures people who are ready to buy. Retargeting ties the two together. If your budget is limited, start with the platform that fits your customers best, prove it works, and then add the second.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Running ads without tracking.</strong> If you cannot see which ads produce enquiries, you cannot improve them, and you will keep paying for the ones that fail.</li>
<li><strong>Sending all traffic to the homepage.</strong> A page built around the specific offer in your ad converts far better than a general homepage.</li>
<li><strong>Changing everything at once.</strong> Adjust one thing at a time, such as the headline, image or audience, so you know what caused any improvement.</li>
<li><strong>Judging results too early.</strong> Give each campaign enough time and budget to collect meaningful data before making big decisions.</li>
<li><strong>Letting someone else own your ad account.</strong> Your account, pixel and audience data are business assets. Always keep ownership.</li>
</ul>
<p>Avoiding these five mistakes will save most small businesses a large share of their first-month budget, and it makes every later improvement easier to measure.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Which is cheaper, Meta Ads or Google Ads?</h3>
<p>Meta Ads usually cost less per click, but Google Ads clicks often come from people closer to buying. The cheaper platform is the one that produces customers at a profitable cost, not the one with the lowest click price.</p>
<h3>How long until ads start working?</h3>
<p>Most campaigns need two to four weeks of data before they can be optimised properly. You may see enquiries earlier, but treat the first month as a learning period.</p>
<h3>Do I need a website to run ads?</h3>
<p>Yes, or at least a dedicated landing page. Ads send people somewhere, and that page decides whether they become a lead. Our <a href="/services/web-development">web development service</a> includes landing pages from $129.</p>

<h2>Putting it into practice</h2>
<p>Choose Google Ads when people already search for what you sell, choose Meta Ads when you need to create awareness or sell something visual, and measure everything from day one. We manage both for you, so see our <a href="/services/paid-advertising">paid advertising management</a> or <a href="/pricing#ads">compare ad management pricing</a>. When you are ready, <a href="/contact">book a free consultation</a> and we will suggest the right starting budget.</p>
HTML,
    ],

